# Plan — etude-5f6: make `etude gate` walk the invocation-fallback ladder

## Why this one matters

This is the defect that stopped this lane. `.etude/registry.yaml` carries an
ordered `invocation_fallbacks` list per seat, and `Seat.Invocations()` returns
primary-then-fallbacks in retry order — but `etude gate` never reads it.
`ResolveGateSeat` builds an `ExecRunner` from `seat.Invoke` alone, so when the
primary invocation fails the seat is recorded `failed` and the gate escalates,
with the configured alternatives untouched.

For the opus seat under a Claude Code supervisor that is exactly the wrong
behaviour: the primary (`claude -p`) cannot authenticate, the next candidate is
the in-harness sub-agent, and the ladder that would have said so is inert. The
config describes a recovery path nothing performs — the same shape as the prose
comment this epic already replaced.

## The two candidate kinds

The ladder mixes two things, and the distinction is load-bearing:

- **exec-able candidates** — a shell command etude can run itself.
- **`in-harness:` candidates** — NOT commands. They name work only the HOST can
  do (spawn a sub-agent). etude must never exec one; a consumer that tried would
  get ENOENT and, correctly but uselessly, record an outage.

## Shape

`ResolveGateSeat` currently returns one runner. It gains a sibling that returns
the ordered candidates, and `runGateSeats` walks them:

1. For each candidate in `Seat.Invocations()` order:
   - if it is `in-harness:`, SKIP it — etude cannot run it — but remember it.
     Do NOT stop the ladder.
   - otherwise exec it. If it yields a USABLE verdict (`go`/`block`), take it and
     stop.
   - if it yields `failed`/`empty`/`malfunction`, record why and continue.
2. If every exec-able candidate is exhausted, the seat is `failed` with a note
   naming each candidate tried and its failure — today's message names only the
   primary — AND naming any `in-harness:` candidate that was skipped, so the
   caller learns there is a path etude cannot take.

**Skip, do not stop, at an `in-harness:` candidate.** An earlier draft stopped
the ladder there; the plan gate caught that the repo's own opus ladder puts `agy`
AFTER the in-harness entry, so stopping would never reach a candidate etude can
actually run. etude cannot know whether its caller is able to spawn a sub-agent —
a Claude Code supervisor can, a codex/gemini orchestrator cannot — so it must not
decide on the caller's behalf. Trying everything it CAN run, and reporting what
it could not, is the only rule that is correct for both callers and never
silently degrades: a usable verdict from a later candidate beats an outage, and
the skipped in-harness path is surfaced rather than swallowed.

**Record which candidate ran.** The registry comment already requires this
("record the candidate that actually ran in session/provider evidence"). The
`SeatResult.Harness` field is the natural home: a fallback carries its own
`harness` (e.g. `agy`), so a run's evidence shows the seat identity AND the
harness that actually produced the verdict. Without that a reader cannot tell a
primary verdict from a fallback verdict.

**`SeatMeta` is per-CANDIDATE, not per-seat.** `requiresSessionEvidence` is
computed from provider AND harness, and the harness changes down the ladder
(`claude-code` → `claude-code-subagent` → `agy`). Resolving it once per seat
would apply the primary's evidence requirement to a fallback that has a
different one. Each candidate gets its own meta.

**`Harness.Name` must be non-empty even when every candidate failed.**
`runmanifest` rejects a seat result with an empty `harness.name`
(`manifest.go:581`), so an exhausted ladder that left the field blank would fail
to WRITE the attempt at all — turning a recorded outage into no record. The
exhausted case carries the seat's declared primary harness.

**An `in-harness:` candidate needs a distinct outcome, not a silent failure.**
If etude simply skipped it the gate would escalate with a misleading "all
candidates failed". Instead the seat result records `failed` with a failure note
naming the candidate and stating that the HOST must run it — so a supervisor
reading the gate output learns what to do rather than that something broke.
Inventing a new `SeatVerdict` is out of scope: the manifest's verdict set is a
schema surface, and `failed` + a precise note is honest and needs no migration.

**The note must be machine-readable, not English.** A supervisor should not have
to string-match prose to detect this case. The note leads with a stable marker
and echoes the candidate verbatim:

    IN_HARNESS_CANDIDATE_SKIPPED harness=<harness> invoke=<invoke>

followed by the human sentence. Same for exhaustion: one
`CANDIDATE_FAILED harness=<h> invoke=<i> verdict=<v>` line per rung rather than a
single concatenated blob, so a reader can tell WHICH rung failed HOW.

## Tests

- Primary succeeds → fallback never invoked (assert the fallback runner is not
  called), and the recorded harness is the primary's.
- Primary fails, fallback succeeds → verdict comes from the fallback and the
  recorded harness is the FALLBACK's, not the seat's default.
- All exec-able candidates fail → seat `failed`, note names every candidate tried.
- `in-harness:` candidate → never exec'd, and the ladder CONTINUES: a later
  exec-able candidate still gets its turn and can supply the verdict. Planned
  regression: `seat_in_harness_candidate_is_skipped_not_terminal`.
- All exec-able candidates fail AND an `in-harness:` candidate was skipped →
  seat `failed`, note names both the failures and the skipped candidate.
- A seat with NO fallbacks behaves exactly as today (no regression for the
  common case).
- Ordering: candidates are tried in `Invocations()` order, not map order.

## One resolver, both callers

`Engine.ResolveSeat` returns a single runner and is used by BOTH `etude gate` and
`etude run`. The ladder is added as an OPTIONAL `ResolveSeatCandidates` field:
when set, `runGateSeats` walks the candidates; when nil it falls back to the
existing single-runner path, so every current caller and test is unchanged.

It is then wired for `etude gate` AND `etude run`, from the same closure. Wiring
only one would leave two code paths resolving seats differently — which is the
"second seat path" this bead's parent explicitly set out to avoid, and it would
mean a seat that recovers under `etude gate` still dies under `etude run` for no
reason a reader could infer. The fallback-to-single-runner behaviour is what
keeps that safe: nothing changes for a seat with no fallbacks configured, which
is every seat except `opus` today.

## What this bead does NOT fix, stated plainly

**It does not make a Claude Code supervisor's gate PASS.** With the in-harness
candidate skipped, the seat is still `failed`, so `synthesizeVerdict` still sees
too few usable seats and still escalates. The lane still needs the human to run
the sub-agent and record the verdict.

What changes is that the ladder is walked and the outcome is actionable: an
exec-able fallback now actually gets its turn, and when none works the record
names the candidate etude could not run, in a greppable form. Closing the loop
needs a way to FEED a host-produced verdict back into the round, which is a
distinct mechanism (verdict injection) and its own bead — filed as **etude-4ed**
so this one is not mistaken for the whole fix.

## Out of scope

- Actually spawning a sub-agent from etude. It cannot; that is the host's job and
  the `in-harness:` marker exists to say so.
- Feeding a host-produced verdict back into a gate round — **etude-4ed**.
- Retry/backoff policy within a single candidate. The registry comment is
  explicit that retry policy belongs to the consumer; this bead adds ordered
  candidate traversal, not retries.
